						"score": 1,
						"text": "reVault is a new, secure-by-default archive format called a lockbox, designed to be simple to use.<p>I&#x27;ve been working on reVault for about 2 years. It was originally written in Dart, but with the improvements in AI, I decided to port it to Rust using AI.<p>It&#x27;s now in beta, so I&#x27;m looking for feedback, particularly around the different language bindings.<p>A lockbox is encrypted, signed and compressed by default. Each can be disabled selectively.<p><pre><code>  - Hybrid post-quantum or passphrase encryption, including metadata.\n  - Zstd compression.\n  - Pure Rust libraries wherever possible.\n</code></pre>\nWe intend to phase out remaining C code as alternatives become available. The project has been scanned using OpenAI&#x27;s Daybreak cyber security tools.<p>The manual is a work in progress:\nhttps:&#x2F;&#x2F;docs.revault.onepub.dev&#x2F;<p>reVault supports Linux, macOS and Windows, x64 and arm64, plus Wasm. The core is Rust, with bindings for C&#x2F;C++, Java, Dart, Python, Ruby, JS&#x2F;TypeScript, Go, C#, Kotlin, Lua, PHP and Swift.<p>Within a lockbox you can store:<p><pre><code>  - Files, directories, permissions and symlinks.\n  - Variables.\n  - Forms.\n</code></pre>\nVariables, forms and the lockbox itself can have descriptions.<p>reVault uses a portable vault (just another lockbox with structured content) to hold your keys, contacts&#x27; keys, lockbox aliases and form definitions.<p>It ties into your platform&#x27;s secure store, so logging into your desktop unlocks your vault. You don&#x27;t have to enter a passphrase every time you access a lockbox. You can disable this.<p>There are lots of shortcuts. Creating a lockbox stores an alias in your vault, based on its filename or a name you choose:<p><pre><code>  lbx create &#x2F;some&#x2F;path&#x2F;mybox.lbox\n  lbx mybox open --duration 1h\n  lbx mybox var set KEY VALUE\n</code></pre>\nSet a default lockbox and you can leave its name out:<p><pre><code>  lbx session default mybox\n  lbx var get KEY\n</code></pre>\nYou can mirror a directory tree into a lockbox and update it when the directory changes:<p><pre><code>  lbx store.lbox mirrors project create --from .&#x2F;project --to &#x2F;projects&#x2F;project\n  lbx store.lbox mirrors update\n</code></pre>\nThe API lets you read, write and seek within files without extracting them to the filesystem. Each lockbox has a table of contents, so extracting a single file from a multi-gigabyte lockbox is fast.<p>Recovery<p>Updates are transactional, with automatic rollback for failed updates. The format is designed for recovery: content is stored in pages, allowing unaffected content to be recovered if part of a lockbox is corrupted.<p>Sharing<p>An encrypted lockbox is more useful if you can share it.<p>reVault has an open-source keyserver for exchanging public keys, with validation through a second trusted channel. It&#x27;s still experimental.<p>OnePub has donated a server to run a single keyserver. If you&#x27;re interested in donating resources, I&#x27;d like to bring up a second redundant server. It&#x27;s pure Rust, uses under 100 MB RSS and supports rate limits.<p>To publish your default profile&#x27;s public key:<p><pre><code>  lbx vault profiles email you@example.com\n  lbx vault profiles publish\n</code></pre>\nGive the publish code to your contact. They can import your key:<p><pre><code>  lbx vault contacts receive &lt;publish-code&gt; &lt;contact-name&gt;\n</code></pre>\nDisplay your profile&#x27;s fingerprint and exchange it through a second trusted channel:<p><pre><code>  lbx vault profiles fingerprint\n</code></pre>\nRepeat the process in the opposite direction to get their key. Once you have it, grant them access to your default lockbox:<p><pre><code>  lbx access grant alice\n</code></pre>\nYou can then email the encrypted lockbox to Alice.<p>To install the CLI and get started:<p><pre><code>  cargo install revault_cli\n  lbx vault init\n  lbx completion install\n</code></pre>\nCompletion installation detects your shell. To diagnose issues:<p><pre><code>  lbx doctor\n</code></pre>\nThe language bindings are published to their respective package repositories.<p>I&#x27;m looking for constructive criticism of the project on its own merits, particularly the APIs and language bindings. I&#x27;m not interested in a debate about using AI.",
						"time": 1792893681,
						"title": "ReVault – new secure archive format",
						"type": "story"
					},
